  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/f6abba797edf9bd83882c31c2c45729fdcdb40226bf95cc10f0f01c90c68b5f1.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $D(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${D}^{\u2032}(x)$ becomes $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
